*Full article:* “The pharmaceutical sector is less hemmed in, but M&A deals are accelerating,” _Investir_ (Les Echos), 13 August 2026.

#link("https://investir.lesechos.fr/actu-des-valeurs/etudes/le-secteur-pharmaceutique-est-moins-entoure-mais-les-operations-de-fusion-acquisition-accelerent-2247014")

#v(1em)

== Context

After several quarters of regulatory pressure and equity underperformance, the *global pharmaceutical sector* began a rebound in late 2025 and consolidated that recovery in the first half of 2026.

== Key points

=== The trigger: the Trump–Pfizer agreement (1 October 2025)

The sector rebound accelerates after the agreement between Donald Trump and Pfizer. The sector continues its equity recovery *through February 2026*.

=== Regulatory visibility returns

- Announced price cuts will ultimately target only *Medicaid* (the most vulnerable patients).
- Large pharmaceutical groups commit to *relocating production to the United States*, with investments of *USD 20 to 50 billion* each.
- These concessions *“helped release the pressure”* on valuations.

=== Consequence: M&A acceleration

*Sébastien Malafosse*, international equity portfolio manager at *Edmond de Rothschild AM*:

#quote[
  After this regain in visibility, the big pharmas felt much more comfortable opening their purses and starting to buy targets, especially in biotech. We saw a strong return of mergers and acquisitions. With many deals above USD 10 billion.
]

=== Reading the headline

The sector is *“less hemmed in”* (less clouded by political and tariff uncertainty) while *M&A activity accelerates*.

